<Base title="Bounded filesystem events in a Rust Git TUI | Forge TUI" description="A source-backed engineering note on keeping a Git TUI responsive with bounded watcher queues, overflow invalidation and linked worktree metadata." lang="en" path="/en/engineering/git-refresh/" hasTranslation={false}>
  <main id="main" class="seo-article shell">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/en/">Forge TUI</a> / Engineering</nav>
    <h1>When a Git TUI must catch up: bounded filesystem events</h1>
    <p class="lead">A terminal Git workspace has two jobs: respond to input and stay consistent with a working tree that other processes can change. A file watcher helps, but processing every notification is not the same as keeping the displayed state correct.</p>
    <p><small>Engineering note based on Forge revision <a href={'https://github.com/creature-studio/forge/commit/' + revision}>{revision}</a>. Prepared with AI assistance. This is a source-based design explanation, not an application performance benchmark.</small></p>
    <section>
      <h2>Notifications are invalidation hints, not a repository history</h2>
      <p>Editors can save a file through temporary files and renames. Build scripts or coding assistants can change many paths in a burst. Treating every filesystem event as a durable, ordered record is a poor basis for a Git status view: the authoritative state is the repository itself.</p>
      <p>Forge's <code>WatchMailbox</code> uses a bounded Tokio MPSC channel. The watcher callback uses <code>try_send</code> instead of waiting for the consumer. When the queue fills, it marks an atomic overflow flag. The consumer can clear the flag with an atomic swap.</p>
      <pre><code>{snippet}</code></pre>
      <p>The mailbox intentionally does not preserve every event under overload. Its contract is to signal that a snapshot may be stale. A consumer must respond to overflow by refreshing authoritative state; the flag does not reconstruct the discarded event. This bounds the number of queued events, not the memory use of the entire application.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href={source + 'crates/forge-app/src/auto_refresh.rs'}>watcher mailbox and filtering implementation</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Git worktree metadata is not always a directory</h2>
      <p>A linked Git worktree may have a <code>.git</code> pointer file instead of a <code>.git/</code> directory. Forge resolves the gitdir pointer and the optional commondir pointer to locate private and shared metadata. That matters because HEAD and the index can be worktree-specific, while refs can live in the shared repository.</p>
      <p>The filter considers relevant metadata and working-tree changes, while excluding access-only events, transient index locks, and object-store or log churn under the project's .git directory. The resulting notification is still only a reason to refresh, not a new Git status value.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Separate the Git read and write paths</h2>
      <p>The <code>GitService</code> facade uses a gix-backed implementation for status and graph reads and a Git CLI backend for operations including writes and blame. Its synchronous read methods are explicitly documented as blocking; they are intended to be called from a blocking worker rather than from a render loop.</p>
      <p>This separation lets UI scheduling and Git operations evolve independently. It does not by itself establish a speed or memory advantage over another Git client.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href={source + 'crates/forge-git/src/lib.rs'}>GitService facade</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Reproduce the mailbox and worktree tests</h2>
      <p>Check out the cited revision in a separate clone and run the focused test module:</p>
      <pre><code>{'git checkout ' + revision + '\n' + reproduce}</code></pre>
      <p>Three focused tests cover overflow recovery, linked-worktree path resolution and filtering Git object churn. The prior Linux development-node run passed these tests. No application refresh latency, CPU savings, Windows or macOS test result is claimed here.</p>
      <p>For a wider real-PTY smoke test, consult <a href={source + 'scripts/smoke-auto-refresh.py'}>smoke-auto-refresh.py</a> and its prerequisites. That script was not rerun for this note.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Takeaway</h2>
      <p>When a UI observes changing state, bound the notification queue, make overflow explicit and refresh authoritative state. The same principle applies whether a developer is typing in another editor or reviewing the output of a coding assistant.</p>
      <p>Explore <a href="/en/features/git/">Forge's Git review workflow</a>, read the <a href="/en/guide/">user guide</a>, or inspect the <a href="https://github.com/creature-studio/forge">open-source project</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
